            <ArticleTitle>[The effect of paclitaxel-eluting stents on restenosis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-23</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">In our study we aimed to investigate the effects of paclitaxel-eluting stent on restenosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixteen porcine were randomly assigned to two groups (n=8 per group): control group animals received conventional stent implantation and study group animals -paclitaxel-eluting stent implantation. Both groups were treated with 300 mg acetylsalicylic acid and 75 mg clopidogrel daily. The degree of neointimal proliferation and effect of drug-eluting stent on restenosis were evaluated 6 weeks after by angiography and intravascular ultrasound (IVUS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Angiographic in-stent restenosis was lower in paclitaxel-eluting stent group (12.50 +/- 7.07% versus 41.25 +/- 28.50%, p=0.001). The IVUS data demonstrated that paclitaxel group animals had larger minimal lumen area (8.76 +/- 1.09 mm2 versus 6.23 +/- 3.10 mm2, p=0.028), smaller mean neointimal proliferation area (1.03 +/- 0.75 mm2 versus 3.55 +/- 2.86 mm2, p=0.01) and mean percent stenosis (10.71 +/- 8.10% versus 36.85 +/- 30.93%, p=0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study suggests that drug-eluting stents may also have a preventive effect for the in-stent restenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
